One common way to introduce vulnerabilities is through **Insecure Deserialization**. When an application uses the standard Java `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize data from an untrusted source (like a cookie or a POST parameter), an attacker can craft a malicious byte stream to execute arbitrary code on the server.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.ServerSocket;

public class DeserializationExample {
    public void handleRequest(InputStream inputStream) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        // This implementation is vulnerable because it blindly deserializes 
        // any object sent by the client, allowing for Remote Code Execution (RCE).
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(inputStream);
        Object obj = ois.readObject(); 
        System.out.println("Received object: " + obj.toString());
    }
}
